{"id":12441,"date":"2026-07-20T14:58:29","date_gmt":"2026-07-20T21:58:29","guid":{"rendered":"https://staging.kosick.com/?p=12441"},"modified":"2026-08-01T16:17:19","modified_gmt":"2026-08-01T23:17:19","slug":"increase-golf-course-revenue","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://staging.kosick.com/increase-golf-course-revenue/","title":{"rendered":"How to Increase Golf Course Revenue, Whether You Sell Rounds or Memberships"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ask two course operators how they make money and you will get two different answers. A public course lives on rounds: greens fees, cart rentals, and the pro shop and food revenue every round pulls with it. A private club lives on memberships: dues, initiation, and the loyalty keeping a member for a decade. Both want the same thing, more revenue, but the path there is not the same. The fastest way to waste a marketing budget is to chase the wrong one for your type of course. Here is how to increase golf course revenue on whichever road you are actually on, drawn from 40 years marketing for Canadian brands.</p>\n\n\n<style>.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kb-btns12441_ac616a-96{gap:var(--global-kb-gap-xs, 0.5rem );justify-content:center;align-items:center;}.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-button{font-weight:normal;font-style:normal;}.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0{margin-right:5px;}.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button{color:#555555;border-color:#555555;}.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button:hover, .wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button:focus{color:#ffffff;border-color:#444444;}.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button::before{display:none;}.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button:hover, .wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn.kt-btns12441_ac616a-96 .kt-btn-wrap-0 .kt-button:focus{background:#444444;}</style>\n<div class=\"wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn kb-buttons-wrap kb-btns12441_ac616a-96\"><style>ul.menu .wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn .kb-btn12441_01e512-60.kb-button{width:initial;}.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn .kb-btn12441_01e512-60.kb-button{color:var(--global-palette3, #1A202C);font-size:16px;font-family:Poppins;font-weight:400;text-transform:uppercase;border-top-left-radius:20px;border-top-right-radius:20px;border-bottom-right-radius:20px;border-bottom-left-radius:20px;border-top-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-top-style:solid;border-right-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-bottom-style:solid;border-left-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-left-style:solid;padding-right:var(--global-kb-spacing-md, 2rem);padding-left:var(--global-kb-spacing-md, 2rem);}@media all and (max-width: 1024px){.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn .kb-btn12441_01e512-60.kb-button{border-top-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-top-style:solid;border-right-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-bottom-style:solid;border-left-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-left-style:solid;}}@media all and (max-width: 767px){.wp-block-kadence-advancedbtn .kb-btn12441_01e512-60.kb-button{border-top-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-top-style:solid;border-right-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-bottom-style:solid;border-left-color:var(--global-palette1, #3182CE);border-left-style:solid;}}</style><span class=\"kb-button kt-button button kb-btn12441_01e512-60 kt-btn-size-standard kt-btn-width-type-auto kb-btn-global-outline kt-btn-has-text-true kt-btn-has-svg-false wp-block-kadence-singlebtn\"><span class=\"kt-btn-inner-text\">Golf Marketing</span></span></div>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The two ways to increase golf course revenue</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nearly every course grows revenue through one of two engines: more rounds at a healthy rate, or more members at a healthy retention rate. Public and semi-private courses lean on the first, private clubs on the second, and plenty of courses run a mix. Before you spend a dollar on marketing, be honest about which engine actually pays your bills, because the tactics, the audience, and even the tone of your marketing change completely depending on the answer. Everything below is organized around that split.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">If you sell rounds: fill the tee sheet at the right price</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For a public course, revenue is a simple equation with a trap in it: rounds multiplied by rate. The trap is discounting your way to a full sheet, which fills tee times while quietly training golfers to wait for a deal. The better path is demand: build a golfer list, market to it consistently, and use pricing to fill the slow times without gutting your peak. Weekday mornings and shoulder seasons are where most public courses leave money on the table, and smart marketing moves those rounds. Our guide to how to fill tee times walks through the tactics in detail. The goal is a full sheet at a rate that protects your margin, not a busy course losing money on every cart.</p>\n\n\n<style>.kb-image12441_46f511-28 .kb-image-has-overlay:after{opacity:0.3;}</style>\n<figure class=\"wp-block-kadence-image kb-image12441_46f511-28 size-large\"><img decoding=\"async\" src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Golf-Marketing-Public-Play-640x960.jpg\" alt=\"Golf Marketing Public Play BC Alberta\" class=\"kb-img wp-image-12445\"/></figure>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">If you sell memberships: grow the base and keep it</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For a private or semi-private club, one new member is worth far more than one round, because the revenue repeats for years. So the math flips from volume to lifetime value: attract the right prospects, convert them, and above all keep them. Retention is the quiet revenue driver most clubs underinvest in, since a member who renews for ten years is worth more than any single-season acquisition push. That means marketing to prospects and to existing members, with an experience and a brand worth staying for. Our approach to golf membership marketing covers acquisition and retention together, because at a club the two are inseparable.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The multiplier both engines share: brand and experience</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whichever engine drives your revenue, one thing lifts both: how your course looks and feels to the people choosing where to play or where to belong. A course with a strong, consistent brand commands higher rates, attracts better members, and gets recommended more often. Golfers pay more, and stay longer, for a place feeling premium and cared for. This is why golf course branding is not a cosmetic exercise, it is a revenue lever pulling on rounds and memberships at the same time.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Stop leaking the revenue you already have</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before chasing new rounds or members, plug the leaks. Slow-day tee sheets, unsold cart fleets, empty event space, a pro shop nobody markets, and members who quietly lapse are all revenue you already earned and let slip away. A single afternoon mapping where your course loses money often surfaces more revenue than a new ad campaign, and it costs nothing. Growth is easier when you are not refilling a leaking bucket.</p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Building a plan around your real driver</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Higher golf course revenue does not come from doing more of everything. It comes from knowing your engine, rounds or memberships, and pointing every marketing dollar at it while protecting your rate and your retention. The courses winning at this treat marketing as a revenue system, not a series of one-off promotions. If you want that system built around your specific course, our golf marketing team does exactly this for public courses and private clubs across Canada.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ready to grow the revenue your course actually runs on? Get in touch and we will build the plan around your driver.</p>\n\n\n<style>.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-column-wrap{align-content:start;}:where(.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-column-wrap) > .wp-block-kadence-column{justify-content:start;}.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-column-wrap{column-gap:var(--global-kb-gap-md, 2rem);row-gap:var(--global-kb-gap-md, 2rem);max-width:700px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;padding-top:50px;padding-bottom:50px;grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-layout-overlay{opacity:0.30;}@media all and (max-width: 1024px){.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-column-wrap{grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}}@media all and (max-width: 767px){.kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 > .kt-row-column-wrap{grid-template-columns:minmax(0, 1fr);}}</style><div class=\"kb-row-layout-wrap kb-row-layout-id12441_e9dd4c-20 alignfull has-theme-palette4-background-color kt-row-has-bg wp-block-kadence-rowlayout\"><div class=\"kt-row-column-wrap kt-has-1-columns kt-row-layout-equal kt-tab-layout-inherit kt-mobile-layout-row kt-row-valign-top\">\n<style>.kadence-column12441_a4ae87-95 > .kt-inside-inner-col{display:flex;}.kadence-column12441_a4ae87-95 > .kt-inside-inner-col,.kadence-column12441_a4ae87-95 > .kt-inside-inner-col:before{border-top-left-radius:0px;border-top-right-radius:0px;border-bottom-right-radius:0px;border-bottom-left-radius:0px;}.kadence-column12441_a4ae87-95 > .kt-inside-inner-col{column-gap:var(--global-kb-gap-sm, 1rem);}.kadence-column12441_a4ae87-95 > .kt-insi